00:04:48,416 --> 00:04:50,220
-a it suddenly appears.

74
00:04:52,413 --> 00:04:55,314
That flag is equivalent to doing

75
00:04:55,774 --> 00:04:57,835
"--all". It's the same.

76
00:04:58,955 --> 00:04:59,555
You can think of

77
00:05:00,255 --> 00:05:04,156
"-a" as a shorthand to call the same option.

78
00:05:04,476 --> 00:05:08,898
You can have multiple flags in different formats when you call programs.

79
00:05:09,678 --> 00:05:10,178
Usually,

80
00:05:10,198 --> 00:05:11,958
the order doesn't matter.

81
00:05:12,038 --> 00:05:13,939
You can give them in any order.

82
00:05:15,099 --> 00:05:20,041
And a lot of the time with these single character flags they can be combined.

83
00:05:20,061 --> 00:05:21,661
So when I do this

84
00:05:22,077 --> 00:05:25,459
is identical to all the commands that I've run previously.

85
00:05:26,680 --> 00:05:29,082
One important thing to note here is:

86
00:05:29,502 --> 00:05:32,144
the shell is not giving us these features.

87
00:05:33,064 --> 00:05:39,049
All these features of parsing the flags to allow you to do the syntax,

88
00:05:39,429 --> 00:05:43,752
is because the ls program is actually doing parsing to allow you to do this.

89
00:05:44,172 --> 00:05:46,834
And that's why when you write shell programs,

90
00:05:47,194 --> 00:05:51,397
you will most often use libraries that automate a lot of this parsing.

91
00:05:51,897 --> 00:05:52,397
for you.

92
00:05:56,459 --> 00:05:58,180
And there are conventions,

93
00:05:58,181 --> 00:05:59,540
so it's really common for

