00:06:00,401 --> 00:06:01,421
-a to mean all,

95
00:06:01,821 --> 00:06:02,902
it's pretty common to have

96
00:06:03,742 --> 00:06:04,763
-h be help,

97
00:06:05,223 --> 00:06:15,788
so you can most often rely on programs having help or version as their options and

98
00:06:18,229 --> 00:06:18,929
I feel like that

99
00:06:19,149 --> 00:06:21,391
covers most things about flags.

100
00:06:21,491 --> 00:06:27,735
The other arguments that you usually get with programs are just strings.

101
00:06:28,395 --> 00:06:29,476
And when,

102
00:06:29,696 --> 00:06:30,196
for example,

