00:15:26,544 --> 00:15:28,624
now we get this kind of error message.

258
00:15:29,044 --> 00:15:32,205
And if we try to capture that error message,

259
00:15:33,166 --> 00:15:34,726
we haven't captured it.

260
00:15:34,766 --> 00:15:37,869
And that's because that didn't go to the standard output of the program,

261
00:15:38,029 --> 00:15:39,311
that went into the standard error.

262
00:15:39,891 --> 00:15:44,977
In order to capture the error we actually have to use different syntax.

263
00:15:45,717 --> 00:15:57,510
But this is convenient because now programs have a way to communicate both of these types of information without them getting mixed into their output.

264
00:15:59,555 --> 00:16:05,539
One common pattern that you might see is like sometimes you want to ignore the output,

265
00:16:05,959 --> 00:16:08,340
and that's why you use this dev null.

266
00:16:08,440 --> 00:16:11,182
So there's like this notion there are like some special files,

267
00:16:11,382 --> 00:16:12,263
dev null for example,

268
00:16:12,303 --> 00:16:13,463
whatever you write to it,

269
00:16:13,503 --> 00:16:14,684
it just disappears.

270
00:16:14,764 --> 00:16:17,626
It actually doesn't get written anywhere.

271
00:16:20,507 --> 00:16:27,031
The next concept that you will very commonly see in the shell is the notion of environment variables.

272
00:16:28,247 --> 00:16:30,388
To define a variable in bash,

273
00:16:30,588 --> 00:16:33,669
you use this syntax, "foo=bar"

274
00:16:34,649 --> 00:16:38,790
and to access it we use the "$foo" syntax.

275
00:16:40,170 --> 00:16:41,711
It is quite important to know that

276
00:16:42,311 --> 00:16:43,831
"foo var"

277
00:16:44,091 --> 00:16:45,832
with spaces is not valid.

278
00:16:47,452 --> 00:16:48,533
When we try to do this,

279
00:16:48,673 --> 00:16:56,355
what we're actually telling to the program is run the program foo with arguments equals and argument var and then

280
00:16:56,915 --> 00:16:58,876
tells us that program foo doesn't exist,

281
00:16:58,916 --> 00:17:01,919
so we have not defined this variable.

282
00:17:02,519 --> 00:17:04,380
We can put many things in variables.

283
00:17:04,381 --> 00:17:04,782
For example,

284
00:17:04,782 --> 00:17:08,823
we can capture the output of programs into variables.

285
00:17:09,404 --> 00:17:18,530
And because there are conventions that usually programs will look at inputs by splitting by new lines,

286
00:17:18,990 --> 00:17:22,413
if we echo this command,

287
00:17:22,473 --> 00:17:25,335
we see everything split by new lines.

288
00:17:25,839 --> 00:17:32,325
And then if we try to run grep on top of this,

289
00:17:33,827 --> 00:17:38,251
we'll see that grep is operating line by line even though we put everything into a variable.

290
00:17:40,974 --> 00:17:50,443
Now some variables are kind of special in that whenever you create a variable it goes into your current local session

291
00:17:51,011 --> 00:17:55,934
But sometimes you want those variables to be passed into other programs that you define.

292
00:17:56,614 --> 00:17:57,835
So here,

293
00:17:57,955 --> 00:17:59,876
like we created foo equals var,

294
00:18:00,156 --> 00:18:07,761
but if we try to create like a debug equals one,

295
00:18:08,361 --> 00:18:10,522
and then we run this program,

296
00:18:10,642 --> 00:18:11,623
which is just saying,

297
00:18:12,443 --> 00:18:15,305
spawn a shell and check the value of debug,

298
00:18:15,845 --> 00:18:16,606
it's not there.

299
00:18:17,146 --> 00:18:18,387
And that's because by default,

300
00:18:18,467 --> 00:18:18,967
the

301
00:18:19,067 --> 00:18:19,988
when we did this,

302
00:18:20,048 --> 00:18:22,429
it was only set for the local shell.

303
00:18:23,350 --> 00:18:25,211
If we actually want to pass that,

304
00:18:25,852 --> 00:18:28,213
we can call it directly,

305
00:18:28,253 --> 00:18:30,215
we can prepend the command with that,

306
00:18:30,595 --> 00:18:33,977
and now when this program gets launched in its environment,

307
00:18:33,997 --> 00:18:35,719
it will have this variable.

308
00:18:36,599 --> 00:18:42,984
The reason why environment variables are useful is because there are some conventions on things,

309
00:18:43,424 --> 00:18:47,567
on some variables that are commonly used.

310
00:18:47,667 --> 00:18:48,207
For example,

311
00:18:48,227 --> 00:18:50,609
if a program wants to save configuration,

312
00:18:51,110 --> 00:18:55,433
it might check the home environment variable.

313
00:18:55,834 --> 00:18:58,996
So whenever you're running a program,

314
00:18:59,116 --> 00:19:02,719
your operating system is setting this home environment variable that says like:

315
00:19:02,759 --> 00:19:03,120
"Oh,

316
00:19:03,120 --> 00:19:04,901
the home folder for this user is here."

317
00:19:05,381 --> 00:19:10,325
So instead of having to run my command and always specifying whatever is my home folder,

318
00:19:11,106 --> 00:19:17,251
the programs can almost often assume that this environment variable is defined and use that for their operation.

319
00:19:18,531 --> 00:19:19,731
Another example is,

320
00:19:20,011 --> 00:19:24,713
yesterday we saw the date command that just tells us what date it is right now.

321
00:19:25,413 --> 00:19:32,515
But for example if we use the tz environment variable and we say actually we're now in Asia,

322
00:19:32,615 --> 00:19:33,115
Tokyo,

323
00:19:33,175 --> 00:19:34,236
and we run date,

324
00:19:34,636 --> 00:19:43,798
we'll see that the output that we get is different because the date command is checking for this environment variable and changing its behavior based on that.

325
00:19:44,399 --> 00:19:45,899
And that it's useful because

326
00:19:46,279 --> 00:19:46,779
For example,

327
00:19:46,819 --> 00:19:48,160
time zone is something many,

328
00:19:48,240 --> 00:19:55,706
many programs need to know and it would be really annoying if you always had to do date and then specify the time zone manually.

329
00:19:55,707 --> 00:20:08,115
So the local variables you create only exist for...

330
00:20:08,116 --> 00:20:09,255
For the shell session.

331
00:20:09,256 --> 00:20:11,417
So like if I do debug equals one and then I do l,

332
00:20:11,418 --> 00:20:14,139
I do like less or whatever and I do like now

333
00:20:15,191 --> 00:20:15,732
debug again,

334
00:20:15,752 --> 00:20:16,312
it's still 1.

335
00:20:17,913 --> 00:20:24,038
It will be set but child processes will not inherit it unless I do export.

336
00:20:24,199 --> 00:20:26,260
Like if I do export debug

337
00:20:26,981 --> 00:20:33,486
1, I'm telling now all the children that are processed that I create should also get this variable.

338
00:20:34,107 --> 00:20:37,670
And now if we go back to this example,

339
00:20:38,591 --> 00:20:40,973
before we were getting nothing here,

340
00:20:41,413 --> 00:20:43,695
but now because we exported the variable

341
00:20:44,407 --> 00:20:44,868
it will get it.

342
00:20:44,868 --> 00:20:48,530
What does "bash -c" do?

343
00:20:48,531 --> 00:20:49,370
"bash-c" says,

344
00:20:49,450 --> 00:20:49,790
"OK,

345
00:20:49,790 --> 00:20:53,793
run a bash shell running whatever I have on the right."

346
00:20:54,253 --> 00:20:56,254
Just like a way of running...

347
00:20:57,907 --> 00:20:59,328
Like if I do echo debug,

348
00:20:59,368 --> 00:21:01,869
it will just execute exactly in my session.

349
00:21:01,909 --> 00:21:07,511
This is a way of creating like a child shell.

350
00:21:09,752 --> 00:21:12,833
The next concept is like we've seen that we can kind of...

351
00:21:12,834 --> 00:21:15,034
We get inputs through environment variables,

352
00:21:15,234 --> 00:21:15,934
arguments,

353
00:21:16,134 --> 00:21:17,315
and the standard input.

354
00:21:17,415 --> 00:21:19,056
We produce output through the standard output.

355
00:21:19,336 --> 00:21:24,758
But there's another output that we also produce which is the return code.

356
00:21:25,458 --> 00:21:27,199
Usually the standard output is just like

357
00:21:27,931 --> 00:21:30,293
bunch of strings you can think of.

358
00:21:31,293 --> 00:21:37,437
But in many cases it's useful to signal whether the command that we ran was successful or not.

359
00:21:38,398 --> 00:21:38,898
So,

360
00:21:39,899 --> 00:21:44,542
the idea here is when we run ls and everything went well,

361
00:21:44,943 --> 00:21:46,824
we can actually check,

362
00:21:47,384 --> 00:21:47,704
okay,

363
00:21:47,704 --> 00:21:48,985
what is the return code?

364
00:21:48,986 --> 00:21:52,347
And the return code is just another string that is a number.

365
00:21:53,028 --> 00:21:54,369
And in this case it's zero.

366
00:21:54,629 --> 00:21:57,351
And then convention is zero means everything went well.

367
00:21:57,775 --> 00:21:59,456
None zero is there was an error.

368
00:22:14,167 --> 00:22:14,787
It will have,

369
00:22:14,867 --> 00:22:15,628
when you prepend,

370
00:22:15,888 --> 00:22:16,448
that has,

371
00:22:16,909 --> 00:22:22,012
sorry,
so the question is what happens if you export and then you use the prefix?

372
00:22:32,847 --> 00:22:43,854
yeah for i project equal to oh debug i see i see in that case i think it should be one because

373
00:22:43,855 --> 00:22:52,340
you i mean i export like like what like negative shares or the like

374
00:23:01,400 --> 00:23:01,900
Yeah,

375
00:23:05,182 --> 00:23:12,766
the idea of export is you want to export it to all the child processes that this shell will create.

376
00:23:20,689 --> 00:23:24,531
So if we delete debug,

377
00:23:25,131 --> 00:23:27,392
and we try to access it in the child process,

378
00:23:27,472 --> 00:23:27,972
it's not there.

379
00:23:28,592 --> 00:23:30,913
If we just set it here,

380
00:23:31,534 --> 00:23:33,154
and we try to access it in the child process,

381
00:23:33,314 --> 00:23:33,815
it's not there.

382
00:23:33,895 --> 00:23:36,156
When we run export,

383
00:23:36,696 --> 00:23:38,416
and we try to access it in the child process,

384
00:23:38,577 --> 00:23:39,357
it is accessed.

385
00:23:39,358 --> 00:23:40,637
It's like the value is set.

386
00:23:40,638 --> 00:23:45,779
The question is perhaps if you now modify debug,

387
00:23:46,460 --> 00:23:51,162
is the modified debug also modified in the subshelf?

388
00:23:51,163 --> 00:23:54,303
Yeah,
I think so because you already have declared that it's exported.

389
00:23:54,675 --> 00:23:55,175
Right?

390
00:23:57,797 --> 00:23:58,297
Yeah.

391
00:24:00,398 --> 00:24:03,120
Yeah,
sorry I was not understanding the question.

392
00:24:03,860 --> 00:24:06,021
So going back to what we're covering,

393
00:24:06,061 --> 00:24:11,884
so when we run ls if it goes well we have this notion of return code.

394
00:24:12,885 --> 00:24:17,888
If we try to ls something that doesn't exist and we check the return code it's actually true.

395
00:24:18,108 --> 00:24:19,208
It's like something went wrong.

396
00:24:19,469 --> 00:24:20,029
What went wrong,

397
00:24:20,209 --> 00:24:23,811
this was not like a folder we could actually list.

398
00:24:26,164 --> 00:24:32,149
Most of the operators in the shell language will rely on return code.

399
00:24:32,150 --> 00:24:32,650
So,

400
00:24:32,949 --> 00:24:33,430
for example,

401
00:24:33,430 --> 00:24:36,292
what we saw yesterday of using ifs and whiles,

402
00:24:36,692 --> 00:24:37,773
almost any control flow,

403
00:24:38,193 --> 00:24:40,055
they will not look at the output of the program.

404
00:24:40,075 --> 00:24:42,577
They will look at whatever return code they produce.

405
00:24:43,258 --> 00:24:46,720
And in many cases it can be fairly useful because,

406
00:24:46,880 --> 00:24:47,380
for example,

407
00:24:47,461 --> 00:24:52,985
grep will produce zero if it finds the pattern and it produces

408
00:24:55,147 --> 00:24:56,948
non-serial if the parent is not found.

409
00:24:57,348 --> 00:25:02,129
So we can use the...

410
00:25:03,749 --> 00:25:04,350
So we...

411
00:25:06,070 --> 00:25:06,530
Yeah,

412
00:25:06,530 --> 00:25:10,471
so if we use the which is kind of like the & operator,

413
00:25:10,611 --> 00:25:14,813
the second program will only run if the first one runs successfully.

414
00:25:16,033 --> 00:25:22,255
And the opposite version of that is if we run the

415
00:25:22,795 --> 00:25:23,175
PyPy

416
00:25:23,175 --> 00:25:24,275
pipe which is OR,

417
00:25:24,356 --> 00:25:28,357
the second command will only run if the previous one fails.

418
00:25:31,819 --> 00:25:38,161
I think that covers most of like return code logic.

419
00:25:38,602 --> 00:25:40,082
Yeah.

420
00:25:40,242 --> 00:25:41,783
Oh -q is by default grep.

421
00:25:42,263 --> 00:25:43,184
If we just run it,

422
00:25:43,864 --> 00:25:47,805
we'll output whatever it's doing.

423
00:25:49,086 --> 00:25:49,586
The

424
00:25:49,746 --> 00:25:51,227
-q is equivalent to saying quiet.

425
00:25:52,607 --> 00:25:53,107
Like run,

426
00:25:53,390 --> 00:25:55,578
try to match but don't give any output.

427
00:25:57,057 --> 00:26:01,961
And the reason why it is helpful is because we might be using the return code of grep.

428
00:26:05,284 --> 00:26:10,048
The last kind of shell-specific concept is the notion of signals.

429
00:26:10,548 --> 00:26:11,048
So,

430
00:26:11,269 --> 00:26:11,589
again,

431
00:26:11,589 --> 00:26:20,236
yesterday we saw this idea that you can have a command that is running for a while and then you can do Ctrl+C and it suddenly stops.

432
00:26:20,916 --> 00:26:26,221
But what is happening behind the scenes for you to send this Ctrl+C and then

433
00:26:27,637 --> 00:26:30,298
the program actually knowing that it has to stop.

434
00:26:31,219 --> 00:26:36,461
So this is happening because we're sending what are called signals.

435
00:26:37,541 --> 00:26:46,105
Signals are a type of software interrupts and what's happening is the shell is sending a signal to the program with a specific code,

436
00:26:46,125 --> 00:26:47,385
and the program is receiving it.

437
00:26:48,226 --> 00:26:49,686
If it has a way to deal with it,

438
00:26:49,766 --> 00:26:51,147
it will run that code.

439
00:26:51,687 --> 00:26:52,207
Otherwise,

440
00:26:52,227 --> 00:26:53,328
it will probably just exit.

441
00:26:57,069 --> 00:26:58,010
we do this,

442
00:26:58,571 --> 00:27:07,939
what's happening is we're sending the second signal and we can actually write programs that do different behaviors based on this.

443
00:27:08,179 --> 00:27:08,780
So for example,

444
00:27:08,880 --> 00:27:11,563
this is a Python program and

445
00:27:12,363 --> 00:27:17,328
Python has the signal library and we can define a handler that says like,

446
00:27:17,368 --> 00:27:17,688
okay,

447
00:27:17,688 --> 00:27:18,409
I got a second,

448
00:27:18,549 --> 00:27:20,611
but I'm actually not going to stop.

449
00:27:21,572 --> 00:27:22,392
And when

450
00:27:23,413 --> 00:27:24,053
When we do this,

451
00:27:24,213 --> 00:27:24,954
we're just saying,

452
00:27:24,994 --> 00:27:26,575
"Okay,
whenever we get a SIGINT,

453
00:27:26,755 --> 00:27:29,016
we're going to execute this function."

454
00:27:29,556 --> 00:27:30,337
And then by default,

455
00:27:30,377 --> 00:27:32,178
this is just going to count numbers.

456
00:27:32,978 --> 00:27:34,959
So if we run this command,

457
00:27:35,199 --> 00:27:36,500
we see that we're counting numbers.

458
00:27:36,620 --> 00:27:44,004
We try to control C and it's not stopping because we have a way to process what's happening and we are deciding to ignore it.

459
00:27:44,364 --> 00:27:46,005
There are many more signals.

460
00:27:46,045 --> 00:27:46,466
For example,

461
00:27:46,466 --> 00:27:47,886
if we do control backslash,

462
00:27:48,006 --> 00:27:49,087
we're sending SIGQUIT,

463
00:27:49,267 --> 00:27:50,648
which is like a more aggressive,

464
00:27:50,768 --> 00:27:52,869
like you should really exit.

465
00:27:53,493 --> 00:27:58,657
And there are even more aggressive ones that can't be handled like SIGKILL.

466
00:27:59,337 --> 00:28:00,938
There's documentation online,

467
00:28:01,438 --> 00:28:03,840
there are many different options that are defined,

468
00:28:04,200 --> 00:28:08,063
but actually in practice you'll only be using a few of them.

469
00:28:09,504 --> 00:28:17,669
One of the reasons why this might be helpful is say for example you have a script that uses some temporary files

470
00:28:18,049 --> 00:28:22,291
and you want to guarantee that even if something goes wrong and someone interrupts you,

471
00:28:22,371 --> 00:28:25,933
you actually clean up those files so they are not lingering.

472
00:28:26,634 --> 00:28:27,954
This is what we're doing here.

473
00:28:28,075 --> 00:28:35,399
This is the same idea of we have a bus function that says like "okay let's clean up the temporary files"

474
00:28:35,539 --> 00:28:41,362
and in case we get like a second or like a SIGTERM then actually call this function.

475
00:28:42,002 --> 00:28:44,324
And in here we're just creating the file,

476
00:28:44,424 --> 00:28:44,924
sleeping,

477
00:28:45,004 --> 00:28:45,804
and then deleting it.

478
00:28:46,585 --> 00:28:47,085
So

479
00:28:49,958 --> 00:28:56,181
If we run this command and then we control C,

480
00:28:56,341 --> 00:29:01,923
we see that we actually run that piece of code that handles the signal.

481
00:29:02,844 --> 00:29:06,585
There are signals that don't just interrupt the program.

482
00:29:06,805 --> 00:29:08,826
Another common one is control Z.

483
00:29:09,526 --> 00:29:11,407
So when we send control Z,

484
00:29:12,268 --> 00:29:15,269
what the shell does is sends like a SIGTSTP

485
00:29:15,785 --> 00:29:16,485
signal that says,

486
00:29:16,705 --> 00:29:18,346
"Okay,
the terminal told you to stop."

487
00:29:18,826 --> 00:29:20,407
So the program gets suspended.

488
00:29:20,467 --> 00:29:22,248
It gets paused.

489
00:29:22,788 --> 00:29:25,869
And now if we check what processes we have,

490
00:29:25,870 --> 00:29:29,771
we can see that there is this process that was suspended.

491
00:29:29,791 --> 00:29:32,412
We can recover it.

492
00:29:32,872 --> 00:29:35,393
We can send it back.

493
00:29:35,954 --> 00:29:39,915
We can also send signals to that process using the kill command.

494
00:29:40,636 --> 00:29:42,957
You might maybe have seen kill.

495
00:29:43,097 --> 00:29:43,797
Kill is

496
00:29:44,513 --> 00:29:46,914
just like a way of sending any type of signal,

497
00:29:46,994 --> 00:29:49,034
not just one to stop the program.

498
00:29:49,295 --> 00:29:55,416
So for example here we can send the continuation signal to this suspended process.

499
00:29:55,977 --> 00:29:59,518
And this is the process ID which is how the OS identifies it.

500
00:30:00,238 --> 00:30:01,938
And if we now check,

501
00:30:02,039 --> 00:30:04,699
now we see it went from suspended to running.

502
00:30:05,300 --> 00:30:07,960
But if we wanted to still interrupt it,

503
00:30:08,601 --> 00:30:09,281
I think actually

504
00:30:10,889 --> 00:30:11,349
It's left,

505
00:30:11,349 --> 00:30:14,731
but now we have another process that is suspended.

506
00:30:15,211 --> 00:30:19,973
And we can interrupt or continue.

507
00:30:22,054 --> 00:30:24,695
And then instead of doing Ctrl+C,

508
00:30:24,956 --> 00:30:27,577
which we can't because it's suspended,

509
00:30:28,097 --> 00:30:31,579
we can just interrupt it.

510
00:30:32,359 --> 00:30:36,201
We interrupted it by sending the signal explicitly through K.

511
00:30:47,225 --> 00:30:49,787
And I think this covers a lot of the abstractions.

512
00:30:51,888 --> 00:30:53,849
There are a lot of conventions that you will see,

513
00:30:54,169 --> 00:30:55,610
like having flags,

514
