771
00:46:00,330 --> 00:46:02,871
And it will search in that path.

772
00:46:03,131 --> 00:46:07,792
The challenge is now if we disconnect and reconnect and we have a new session,

773
00:46:08,553 --> 00:46:10,093
if we echo path now,

774
00:46:10,513 --> 00:46:12,334
we have lost that modification.

775
00:46:12,534 --> 00:46:13,334
That modification,

776
00:46:13,994 --> 00:46:17,035
even though we exported it for child processes,

777
00:46:17,571 --> 00:46:18,772
it's not getting persisted,

778
00:46:18,872 --> 00:46:22,815
like new sessions do not know about that export.

779
00:46:23,476 --> 00:46:29,801
The way to actually persist that configuration is to add it to the shell configuration file.

780
00:46:30,282 --> 00:46:33,384
That will depend exactly on which shell you're running.

781
00:46:33,484 --> 00:46:45,394
In the remote server we're running bash so if we look at the bash configuration file which is bashrc or bash_profile depending on

782
00:46:46,463 --> 00:46:49,164
the shell you're running.

783
00:46:50,304 --> 00:46:51,424
Here it's pretty basic,

784
00:46:51,585 --> 00:46:58,226
but the main idea is if we include the export path in here.

785
00:46:58,727 --> 00:47:01,687
Now whenever a shell process is created,

786
00:47:02,068 --> 00:47:03,988
it's going to read this file,

787
00:47:04,248 --> 00:47:06,449
it's going to evaluate this line,

788
00:47:06,729 --> 00:47:09,030
and then path will have this entry.

789
00:47:09,810 --> 00:47:12,671
So we can verify this by disconnecting.

790
00:47:12,831 --> 00:47:14,051
We can connect again.

791
00:47:15,023 --> 00:47:16,503
And then if we echo path,

792
00:47:16,743 --> 00:47:20,845
we can verify that it has that extra entry that otherwise it wouldn't have.

793
00:47:21,865 --> 00:47:24,726
And the path is a special variable.

794
00:47:25,026 --> 00:47:30,827
There are variables that modify other parts of the behavior of your shell.

795
00:47:31,608 --> 00:47:34,848
So for example here we have ps1.

